#
# Degrade the robot's ability to move, in physics rather than in software.
#
# Served as /faults/set_mobility_fault by ral_faults/fault_manager.py
# (Phase 4.3). Injection point: /world/<world>/wrench/persistent (gz physics)
# - a persistent wrench on the base link, so the commanded velocity and the
# achieved velocity genuinely diverge and the controller has to cope with a
# plant that no longer does what it is told.
#
# This is deliberately NOT a modification of /cmd_vel: intercepting the
# command would change what the robot was asked to do, which is a different
# fault and would make the commanded-vs-achieved comparison meaningless.
#
# `enabled` false removes the wrench; the remaining fields are then ignored.
# Disabling when no wrench is in force is a FAILURE, not a no-op. A disable
# succeeds only when the clear was PUBLISHED on /world/<world>/wrench/clear
# with the gz bridge subscribed - the same evidence `activated` rests on for
# this kind. Without a subscriber the manager answers success=false, publishes
# `rejected`, and KEEPS the fault in /faults/active: a wrench that cannot be
# shown to have been removed must be assumed still in force. A disable
# publishes `cleared` for the fault it removed and NO event of its own (the
# kind is a singleton, so the disable is not itself something in force); its
# id appears in that event's detail and in the service response.
# `force_x_n`/`force_y_n` are newtons in the BODY frame, `torque_z_nm` is a
# yaw torque in newton-metres - e.g. a torque alone models a dragging wheel.
# All three must be finite; no magnitude bound is imposed, because any finite
# wrench is one gz can apply and an implausibly large one is a visible result
# rather than an invalid request.
# The manager re-projects the body-frame force into the WORLD frame, the only
# frame ApplyLinkWrench accepts, and re-projects it again as the robot turns.
#
# `fault_id` is the join key for this fault instance: the value the caller will
# see again on every FaultEvent the manager publishes for it. The scenario
# runner sends the id it minted (<kind>_<1-based index after sorting by
# at_sec>, scenario.py:308). An EMPTY string means "allocate one", which is
# what the dashboard sends; the manager answers with <kind>_ui_<n>. The
# RESPONSE carries the id the manager used - the one sent, or the minted one
# when the request left it empty.
#
# `success` is true only once the wrench was published with the gz bridge
# subscribed. gz acknowledges a wrench publication with nothing at all, so
# that - and not an accepted entity - is what `activated` can mean here.

string fault_id
bool enabled
float32 force_x_n
float32 force_y_n
float32 torque_z_nm
---
string fault_id
bool success
string message
